Title: The Keeper's Tin
Credit: Written by
Author: A. Writer
Draft date: 2026-10-16
Contact: writer@example.com

FADE IN:

INT. LIGHTHOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT

The kettle rattles on the stove. MARA (60s, oilskin coat, hands like rope) counts coins into a tin, loses count, starts again. Outside the wind leans on the shutters like a tired creditor.

MARA
Eleven, twelve. The sea doesn't take a deposit, and neither does the bank. Count them again, Tom.

TOM
(from the stairs)
You said you would sleep. You said it twice, once with a straight face. The storm will not care whether you counted right, and neither will I.

EXT. CLIFF PATH - CONTINUOUS

Wind flattens the grass. TOM (30s, wet to the knees) hauls a crate up the last steps and sets it down like it might bite.

TOM
Radio parts. The man in the harbour wouldn't take cash. He wanted the clock instead. I told him no. I told him twice. Then I told him a third time, louder.

MARA (O.S.)
My father's clock? Over my dead body. Bring it back, Tom, or I will ring the bell myself and wake the whole coast. Is that understood? Say it.

CUT TO:

INT. LIGHTHOUSE LAMP ROOM - LATER

The great lens turns, slow as a thought. Mara runs a thumb along the brass rail and finds it warm. She looks at Tom. Tom looks at the floor, at the stairs, at anything else.

MARA
It's been lit. Somebody lit it before we got here.

TOM
Nobody has the key but us. Nobody has climbed these stairs since the winter we stopped talking, and that is a long time to keep a secret warm. It was not me. I swear it. It was not me.

EXT. SEA - NIGHT

A small boat, no lights, rides the swell toward the rocks. A figure at the tiller raises one hand to the beam, as if greeting an old friend.

FIGURE
(shouting)
Keep it burning! Whatever they tell you, keep it burning until the morning tide turns. I'll be back before it does. I swear it on the clock. Do you hear me up there? On the clock!

INT. LIGHTHOUSE KITCHEN - DAWN

The storm has gone. Mara sleeps at the table with her head on the tin. The clock on the wall, which had been stopped for thirty years, ticks. Tom stands in the doorway and does not wake her.

TOM
Eleven, twelve. And one more.

CUT TO:

